1
00:00:00,589 --> 00:00:03,970
Imagine a man who put Death in chains.

2
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:07,590
A man who fooled the gods not once…
but twice.

3
00:00:08,170 --> 00:00:13,110
And who paid for it… forever,
pushing a giant boulder up a hill.

4
00:00:14,630 --> 00:00:18,140
Every time he nears the top,
the boulder rolls back down.

5
00:00:18,370 --> 00:00:21,180
So he walks down… and begins again.

6
00:00:22,430 --> 00:00:24,610
His name… Sisyphus.

7
00:00:26,890 --> 00:00:29,470
So why did the gods punish him like this?

8
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:34,410
And why, thousands of years later,
did a thinker call him "happy"?

9
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:40,230
The story begins on a narrow
strip of land between two seas.

10
00:00:40,660 --> 00:00:43,310
Ephyra… known today as Corinth.

11
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:46,780
Its king was Sisyphus.

12
00:00:47,010 --> 00:00:51,380
They say he built up its ships and
its trade, and made his city rich.

13
00:00:53,050 --> 00:00:55,770
But what truly made him
famous was his mind.

14
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,820
Homer calls him "the craftiest of men."

15
00:01:00,510 --> 00:01:03,290
His neighbor, Autolycus,
was a famous thief.

16
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:08,840
So skilled, he could even change the
color of the animals he stole. Each night,

17
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:13,360
Sisyphus's herd grew a little smaller,
but no one could prove a thing.

18
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:18,820
So Sisyphus carved tiny letters under
his cattle's hooves. As they walked,

19
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:22,620
they wrote a sentence in the earth:
"Autolycus stole me."

20
00:01:24,290 --> 00:01:26,870
The tracks led straight
to the thief's barn.

21
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:31,380
But that same cleverness would one
day make him a target of the gods.

22
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:39,160
One day, watching the sky from the heights
of his city, Sisyphus saw something.

23
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:44,000
A giant eagle…
carrying a young girl in its talons.

24
00:01:45,030 --> 00:01:49,220
The eagle was Zeus in disguise.
And the girl was Aegina,

25
00:01:49,370 --> 00:01:51,530
daughter of the river god Asopus.

26
00:01:52,559 --> 00:01:55,569
The next day,
Asopus went searching for his daughter.

27
00:01:55,879 --> 00:02:00,410
He wandered over mountains and plains…
and at last, he came to Corinth.

28
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:01,880
Sisyphus smiled:

29
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:06,700
"I know who took your daughter.
But first… give my city a spring."

30
00:02:07,470 --> 00:02:12,740
Asopus made fresh water burst from the
rocks. And Sisyphus told him the secret.

31
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:18,480
The city had its water. But Sisyphus
had betrayed the king of the gods.

32
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:21,960
And Zeus… never forgot.

33
00:02:24,450 --> 00:02:31,210
Zeus sent his most terrible messenger
to Sisyphus: Death himself… Thanatos.

34
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:36,160
Thanatos appeared at the palace door,
carrying heavy chains.

35
00:02:37,310 --> 00:02:42,800
They say Sisyphus neither ran nor begged.
He simply looked at the chains, curious.

36
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:46,370
"How do these go on? Could you show me?"

37
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:51,800
Thanatos put the chains on his own wrists…
and Sisyphus snapped the lock shut.

38
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:55,160
Death was in chains.

39
00:02:55,950 --> 00:03:00,770
No one in the world died anymore. Soldiers
with fatal wounds got back on their feet,

40
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:04,770
and not a single sacrifice
could be made at the altars.

41
00:03:04,850 --> 00:03:10,390
Ares, the god of war, was furious. What
was the point of a war where no one died?

42
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:13,110
Ares freed Thanatos from his chains…

43
00:03:13,190 --> 00:03:17,000
and handed Sisyphus over to
Death with his own hands.

44
00:03:19,380 --> 00:03:24,770
But before he died, Sisyphus had whispered
something in the ear of his wife, Merope.

45
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:28,370
"Don't bury my body.
Hold no funeral for me.

46
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:29,880
Make no offerings."

47
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:35,370
In the underworld, Sisyphus came
before Persephone, queen of the dead.

48
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:43,110
"My wife has disrespected me! No funeral,
no offerings… Let me go up and punish her.

49
00:03:43,190 --> 00:03:44,560
I'll come right back."

50
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:46,880
Persephone believed him.

51
00:03:48,110 --> 00:03:53,180
Sisyphus saw the sun again.
The sea, the wine, his city…

52
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:54,980
And he never went back.

53
00:03:55,060 --> 00:03:56,680
He lived on for years.

54
00:03:56,760 --> 00:04:01,770
Until Hermes, messenger of the gods,
came and dragged him back by force.

55
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:07,700
This time, the gods prepared a
special punishment for Sisyphus.

56
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:14,980
Deep in Tartarus, a steep hill.
At the foot of the hill, a giant boulder.

57
00:04:15,320 --> 00:04:18,800
His task was simple:
get the boulder to the top.

58
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:21,692
He set his shoulder to the stone.
He pushed.

59
00:04:21,977 --> 00:04:26,190
His feet slipped, his muscles shook…
one step from the top, and then…

60
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:31,310
The boulder broke free. And with a roar,
it rolled all the way to the bottom.

61
00:04:33,230 --> 00:04:36,330
Sisyphus walked down. And began again.

62
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:41,440
The next day, too. And the day after that.

63
00:04:41,730 --> 00:04:45,270
A hundred years later…
a thousand years later.

64
00:04:45,470 --> 00:04:47,430
Because the gods knew one thing:

65
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:52,210
there is no more dreadful punishment
than futile and hopeless labor.

66
00:04:55,510 --> 00:04:57,970
More than two thousand years went by.

67
00:04:58,360 --> 00:05:01,710
In nineteen forty-two,
with the world at war once again,

68
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:06,460
a young writer named Albert Camus looked
at this story with completely new eyes,

69
00:05:06,540 --> 00:05:09,060
in his essay, The Myth of Sisyphus.

70
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:14,030
For Camus,
there is a little Sisyphus in all of us.

71
00:05:14,220 --> 00:05:16,380
Every morning we get up, we work,

72
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:21,650
we walk the same streets… and one day,
we ask ourselves: "What is all this for?"

73
00:05:24,030 --> 00:05:27,690
The universe gives no answer.
Camus calls this rift,

74
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:32,540
between the human need for meaning and
the silence of the world, "the absurd."

75
00:05:33,970 --> 00:05:38,330
But what truly fascinated Camus was not
the climb. It was the way back down,

76
00:05:38,410 --> 00:05:43,490
after the boulder falls, as Sisyphus
descends with heavy but steady steps.

77
00:05:45,070 --> 00:05:50,180
That return is like a breath. For Camus,
it is the hour of consciousness.

78
00:05:50,510 --> 00:05:53,570
In that moment,
Sisyphus is fully aware of his fate.

79
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:57,750
And precisely because of that…
he is stronger than his rock.

80
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:02,290
The rock is his. His fate belongs to him.

81
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,120
The gods gave him no meaning…
but they could not crush him either.

82
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:09,500
Camus ends his essay with these words:

83
00:06:10,650 --> 00:06:14,940
"The struggle itself toward the heights
is enough to fill a man's heart."

84
00:06:18,190 --> 00:06:20,980
"One must imagine Sisyphus happy."

85
00:06:24,290 --> 00:06:25,830
Maybe we all have a rock.

86
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:32,180
A job, a responsibility, a dream…
or simply, starting over every morning.

87
00:06:33,870 --> 00:06:36,470
Maybe the point isn't
getting the rock to the top.

88
00:06:36,760 --> 00:06:39,990
It's choosing who we become
while we put our shoulder to it.

89
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:44,450
And you? What is your rock?

90
00:06:46,230 --> 00:06:47,530
Whatever your rock is,

91
00:06:47,610 --> 00:06:50,330
tell us in the comments.
See you in the next story.

